  <exercise>
  <introduction>
   

    <p>
      A popular cruise ship sets sail in the Gulf of Mexico with
      <m>5000</m> passengers and crew on board. Unfortunately, five family
      members who board the ship are carrying a highly contagious virus.
      After interacting with many other passengers in the first few hours of
      the cruise, all five of them get very sick. Let <m>S(t)</m> be the
      number of people who have acquired the virus <m>t</m> days after the
      ship has left port. It turns out that a logistic function is a good
      model for <m>S</m> and thus we assume that
      <md>
        <mrow>S(t) = \dfrac{A}{1+Me^{-kt}}</mrow>
      </md>
      for some positive constants <m>A</m>, <m>M</m>, and <m>k</m>. Suppose
      that after <m>1</m> day, <m>20</m> people have gotten the virus.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall we know that <m>S(0) = 5</m> and <m>S(1)=20</m>. In addition,
        assume that <m>5000</m> is the number of people who will eventually
        get sick (that is everyone on the cruise!). Use this information to determine the exact values of
        <m>A</m>, <m>M</m>, and <m>k</m> in the logistic model.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many days will it take for <m>4000</m> of the people on the
        cruise ship to have acquired the virus?  Show your setup clearly, 
        show clear computations, and your final answer should be extremely
        clear (like "It will take .... days for ....")
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the average rate of change of <m>S</m> on the intervals
        <m>[1,2]</m>, <m>[3,4]</m>, and <m>[5,7]</m>. Show your computations carefully.
 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.6in">
    <statement>
      <p>
               What is the meaning of
        each of the values in (c) (with units) in the context of the question, and
        what trend(s) do you observe in these average rates of change?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>


</worksheet>